 expression inside the square root in \\( p(x) \\) is non-negative. The function \\( p(x) = \\sqrt{-x} \\) is defined for \\( -x \\geq 0 \\), which simplifies to \\( x \\leq 0 \\). Therefore, for \\( p(q(x)) = \\sqrt{-q(x)} \\) to be defined, we need \\( -q(x) \\geq 0 \\), or equivalently, \\( q(x) \\leq 0 \\).\n\nFirst, we need to find the roots of the quadratic equation \\( q(x) = 8x^2 + 10x - 3 = 0 \\). We use the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 8 \\), \\( b = 10 \\), and \\( c = -3 \\).\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-10 \\pm \\sqrt{10^2 - 4 \\cdot 8 \\cdot (-3)}}{2 \\cdot 8} = \\frac{-10 \\pm \\sqrt{100 + 96}}{16} = \\frac{-10 \\pm \\sqrt{196}}{16} = \\frac{-10 \\pm 14}{16}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-10 + 14}{16} = \\frac{4}{16} = \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-10 - 14}{16} = \\frac{-24}{16} = -\\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nSo, the roots of the quadratic equation are \\( x = \\frac{1}{4} \\) and \\( x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\). The quadratic function \\( 8x^2 + 10x - 3 \\) is a parabola opening upwards, so it is non-positive between its roots. Therefore, \\( q(x) \\leq 0 \\) for \\( -\\frac{3}{2} \\leq x \\leq \\frac{1}{4} \\).\n\nThe domain of \\( p(q(x)) \\) is thus \\( -\\frac{3}{2} \\leq x \\leq \\frac{1}{4} \\). The length of this interval is:\n\n\\[\nb - a = \\frac{1}{4} - \\left( -\\frac{3}{2} \\right) = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{6}{4} = \\frac{7}{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{7}{4}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{7}{4}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:495"}
